<!--
  Backups and recovery. Facts: packages/basecamp/docs/backups.md, the header of
  api/src/jobs/backup-run.job.ts and app-drill-run.job.ts, HubConfig in
  db/schema.lite, and web/test/verify-hub-restore.mjs for the proof's hops.
  Retention is stated for the hub's archives only: that is the one prune
  that exists.
-->
<section id="backups" class="bc-section">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Backups and recovery</p>
    <h2>Lose the box. Get the data back.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Basecamp archives itself and every app it runs into a bucket you own, prunes the
      pile for you, and restores a hub from the setup page of a fresh install. You can
      run that restore on a scratch box today, before the day you need it.
    </p>

    <div class="bc-split">
      <ul class="bc-points">
        <li>
          <strong>Two kinds of archive, both off the machine.</strong>
          The hub's archive holds every database its schema declares, the audit trail
          and the job queue, each taken with <code>VACUUM INTO</code> so a write in
          flight cannot tear it. An app's archive is its data volume, tarred by the
          Outpost on its machine, on a cron per app and before any release of an
          app that has a volume.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A bucket you own, checked when you save it.</strong>
          Any S3-compatible bucket works; Backblaze B2 is the worked example and
          Cloudflare R2 the runner-up. Adding one writes and deletes a probe key at
          once, so a wrong key fails at the form, in words, not at 2 a.m. The hub
          keeps seven archives on disk and seven in the bucket by default, with an
          optional age limit, and never prunes the newest good one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Restore is a page, not a runbook you write during the outage.</strong>
          On a new box with the old <code>ENCRYPTION_KEY</code>, <code>/setup</code> lists
          the archives in your bucket. Pick one and it is checked before a file is
          touched: a wrong key, a damaged file or a schema this version cannot carry
          on from is refused with its reason. Restart, and the old hub's owner signs in.
          The key is not in the archive, so Basecamp grades the hub red until you
          attest that you kept a copy off the box.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A drill that opens the copy, not just counts its bytes.</strong>
          An app with a live replica can be drilled on a schedule or by hand: the
          machine restores the replica into a scratch directory and runs the app's own
          release image over it, with no network, to verify it. The app's volume is
          never touched, and each answer is kept as a row.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure class="bc-proof">
        <div class="flow" role="list">
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Back up</strong> hub archive lands in the bucket</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Lose it</strong> new box, same key</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>/setup</strong> List archives</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Restore this</strong> checked, then staged</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Restart</strong> the page notices, you sign in</span>
          <span class="arrow">→</span>
          <span class="hop" role="listitem"><strong>Replicas found</strong> each app offers its restore</span>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The path <code>bun run verify:hub-restore</code> drives in a browser: a hub backed up
          to a SeaweedFS bucket, restored at <code>/setup</code> across a restart, then its
          app and its own replicas found. From
          <code>packages/basecamp/web/test/verify-hub-restore.mjs</code> and
          <code>docs/backups.md</code> § 7. Proved against a local S3 stand-in; a live run
          on a real bucket is still owed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p class="bc-more">
      Read more:
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/basecamp/docs/backups.md">the backups recipe</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/basecamp/api/src/jobs/app-drill-run.job.ts">what a drill proves</a>,
      <a href="https://github.com/frontierjs/frontierjs/blob/main/packages/basecamp/web/test/verify-hub-restore.mjs">the restore drive</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .flow { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch; gap: .3rem; font-size: .9rem; }
  .flow .hop { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .1rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: .75rem; padding: .5rem .9rem; background: var(--surface-raised); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .flow .hop strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .flow .arrow { color: var(--ink-mute); text-align: center; line-height: 1; }
</style>
